   "title": "Hyperbaton in Góngora",
   "body": "<p>In ordinary English a subject can be separated from its verb by an insertion: “The doctor, who had driven all night through the snow to reach the village, finally arrived.” The hearer keeps the subject in mind and waits for the verb. A poet can make the wait longer. The separation of words that belong together is the figure that the grammarians call <strong>hyperbaton</strong>, and we have seen that Quintilian counts it among the figures. Góngora uses it boldly in the first lines of his <i>Soledad primera</i> (“First Solitude”), where the subject stands in the second line and its verb in the sixth:</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Era del año la estacion florida</i><br><i lang=\"es\">En que el mentido robador de Europa</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Media luna las armas de su frente,</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Y el sol todos los rayos de su pelo,</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Luciente honor del cielo</i><br><i lang=\"es\">En campos de zafiro pace estrellas,</i></p><p>“It was the flowering season of the year / in which the false abductor of Europa, / a half moon the arms of his brow, / and the sun all the rays of his hair, / shining honor of the sky, / in fields of sapphire grazes on stars.”</p><p>The subject is <i lang=\"es\">el mentido robador de Europa</i>, “the false abductor of Europa,” that is, Jupiter in the shape of a bull (<i lang=\"es\">mentido</i> is “false” in the Academy's dictionary). Its verb is <i lang=\"es\">pace</i> (“grazes,” said of cattle; Academy, <i lang=\"es\">pacer</i>). Between them stand two clauses with no verb, <i lang=\"es\">media luna las armas de su frente</i> and <i lang=\"es\">y el sol todos los rayos de su pelo</i>, and the apposition <i lang=\"es\">luciente honor del cielo</i>. In the order of ordinary prose the subject and its verb stand together: <i lang=\"es\">el mentido robador de Europa pace estrellas en campos de zafiro</i>. The reader sees the figure by comparing the poet's order with this one. Spanish makes the long wait possible, because the ending of <i lang=\"es\">pace</i> shows that its subject is in the third person singular, as we saw in the free order of the Spanish sentence.</p>",
