   "title": "Antithesis in Quevedo",
   "body": "<p>English has familiar sayings built on opposition: “United we stand, divided we fall.” The two halves match in form, and the saying gets its force from the opposition of “united” and “divided,” and of “stand” and “fall.” The setting of two opposed ideas in matching form is <strong>antithesis</strong> (Greek <i>antithesis</i>, “a setting against”). The Academy's dictionary defines it as the opposition of a word or phrase to another of contrary meaning (<i lang=\"es\">oposición de una palabra o una frase a otra de significación contraria</i>; entry <i lang=\"es\">antítesis</i>). Quevedo's sonnet on the shortness of life (<i lang=\"es\">Represéntase la brevedad de lo que se vive</i>) sets yesterday against tomorrow in a single line, and then places the speaker between them:</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Ayer se fué, mañana no ha llegado,</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Hoy se está yendo sin parar un punto,</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Soy un fué, y un será y un es cansado.</i></p><p>“Yesterday went away, tomorrow has not arrived, / today is going away without stopping a moment, / I am a was, and a will be and a tiring is.”</p><p>In the first line each half has the word for the day first and its verb after it: <i lang=\"es\">ayer</i> is set against <i lang=\"es\">mañana</i>, and <i lang=\"es\">se fué</i> (“went away”) against <i lang=\"es\">no ha llegado</i> (“has not arrived”). No word is carried away from its proper sense, because <i lang=\"es\">ayer</i> is the day before and <i lang=\"es\">mañana</i> the day after, and so the figure lies in the arrangement and not in any single word. Antithesis, therefore, is not a trope. The third line makes the verb forms <i lang=\"es\">fué</i>, <i lang=\"es\">será</i>, and <i lang=\"es\">es</i> into nouns with the article <i lang=\"es\">un</i>, and so names the three times of the speaker's life; <i lang=\"es\">cansado</i> is “tiring” (Academy, <i lang=\"es\">cansado</i>, 3), and <i lang=\"es\">punto</i> is “moment” (Academy, <i lang=\"es\">punto</i>, 25).</p>",
