   "id": "figures-3",
   "title": "The proverb in the <i>Celestina</i>, and the text and its spelling",
   "body": "<p>English speakers answer a doubt about a person's friends with a proverb, “Birds of a feather flock together.” A proverb is a short saying in common use that states a general truth and is applied to the case at hand. The Academy's dictionary defines the Spanish <i lang=\"es\">refrán</i> as <i lang=\"es\">dicho agudo y sentencioso de uso común</i> (“a pointed and sententious saying in common use”). In the <i>Celestina</i> (1499) a character quotes a proverb at the moment when it serves. Calisto, who loves Melibea, has received from the old go-between Celestina a girdle (<i lang=\"es\">cordón</i>) that Melibea wore, and he complains that however much he labors night and day, it does him no good. Celestina answers:</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Refrán viejo es: quien menos procura, alcança mas bien. Pero yo te haré procurando conseguir lo que siendo negligente no haurías. Consuélate, señor, que en vna hora no se ganó Çamora; pero no por esso desconfiaron los combatientes.</i></p><p>“It is an old Proverbe; He that labours least, often-times gets most. But I will make thee by thy labouring, to obtaine that which by being negligent, thou shouldst never atchieve. For Zamora was not wonne in an houre; yet did not her besiegers for all this despaire.” (<i>Celestina</i>, Act VI)</p><p>Celestina names the saying a proverb, <i lang=\"es\">refrán viejo</i>, and it states a general truth: <i lang=\"es\">quien menos procura, alcança mas bien</i>, he who strives least obtains the more good (<i lang=\"es\">procurar</i> is “to strive for,” and <i lang=\"es\">alcanzar</i> is “to obtain”). She then turns it to Calisto's case: she will make him obtain by striving what he would not obtain by negligence. A second proverb follows, <i lang=\"es\">en vna hora no se ganó Çamora</i>, “Zamora was not won in an hour,” and she applies it as well: the besiegers of the city did not despair because it was not taken at once, and so Calisto should not despair.</p><p>The Spanish is quoted from the edition of Julio Cejador (1913), which says that it reproduces the first edition, printed at Burgos in 1499, with full fidelity, and prints in italic type what the later edition of Valencia (1514) adds; this passage is all in roman type, and so it is the text of 1499. The edition keeps the letters of the early printings: <i lang=\"es\">v</i> where today <i lang=\"es\">u</i> is written, in <i lang=\"es\">vna</i>; <i lang=\"es\">ç</i> where today <i lang=\"es\">z</i> is written, in <i lang=\"es\">alcança</i> and <i lang=\"es\">Çamora</i>; <i lang=\"es\">ss</i> in <i lang=\"es\">esso</i>, today <i lang=\"es\">eso</i>; and <i lang=\"es\">u</i> where today <i lang=\"es\">b</i> is written, in <i lang=\"es\">haurías</i>, today <i lang=\"es\">habrías</i>. It writes accents in <i lang=\"es\">refrán</i>, <i lang=\"es\">haré</i>, and <i lang=\"es\">ganó</i>, but it writes <i lang=\"es\">mas</i> without one, where the Academy writes <i lang=\"es\">más</i> today. An editor who modernizes may change the spelling, the accents, and the punctuation, but he may not change the words or their order. This edition does not modernize the letters, and so the quotations of this deck keep every letter and mark of the edition, even where the edition differs from the spelling of today, as the edition of Góngora prints <i lang=\"es\">estacion</i> without an accent. The English of Mabbe is quoted as the reprint of 1894 prints it, in the spelling of 1631.</p>",
