   "id": "figures-4",
   "title": "A change of line length",
   "body": "<p>A poem often keeps one length of line, but it need not. In the dedication of the <i>Soledades</i> to the Duke of Béjar, Góngora begins with four lines of unequal length:</p><p><i lang=\"es\">Pasos de un peregrino son errante</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Cuantos me dictó versos dulce musa,</i><br><i lang=\"es\">En soledad confusa</i><br><i lang=\"es\">Perdidos unos, otros inspirados.</i></p><p>“Steps of a pilgrim are wandering, / as many verses as a sweet muse dictated to me, / in confused solitude / some lost, others inspired.” In the plain order of prose the sense is: “As many verses as a sweet muse dictated to me, some lost in confused solitude, others inspired, are the steps of a wandering pilgrim” (<i lang=\"es\">errante</i> is “wandering”; Academy, <i lang=\"es\">errante</i>).</p><p>We have seen that Spanish verse counts syllables, joining a final vowel to a vowel that follows. The first line has eleven: <i lang=\"es\">Pa-sos-deun-pe-re-gri-no-son-e-rran-te</i>, with <i lang=\"es\">de un</i> joined into one syllable. The second has eleven: <i lang=\"es\">cuan-tos-me-dic-tó-ver-sos-dul-ce-mu-sa</i>. The third has seven: <i lang=\"es\">en-so-le-dad-con-fu-sa</i>. The fourth has eleven again: <i lang=\"es\">per-di-dos-u-nos-o-tros-ins-pi-ra-dos</i>. A poem of this kind, with no stanzas, in which lines of seven and eleven syllables alternate freely, is a <i lang=\"es\">silva</i> (Academy, <i lang=\"es\">silva</i>, 2). Góngora's first <i>Solitude</i> has the same mixture, and its fifth line, <i lang=\"es\">Luciente honor del cielo</i>, has seven syllables among lines of eleven.</p><p>A change of line length is not a trope. A trope changes the signification of a word, whereas a line of seven syllables and a line of eleven can both be literal. So the varied length of the lines is a fact of meter, and not a figure of meaning.</p>",
